I wrote the two task files and the issue responses to session_plan/. The commit step was a no-op because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The files are on disk, which is where the implement phase reads them.

Task 1: Fixes #1234 (product, new issue I filed at planning). When yoyo -p can't write its reply to stdout, for example on a full disk, text and stream-json exit 0 with no message and json mode panics. The fix makes all three print error writing to stdout: … and exit 1, which is what -o already does. The task file says:
• Leave the deliberate SIGPIPE behaviour alone, so yoyo -p … | head still dies quietly.
• Don't add lines to src/format/mod.rs or src/context.rs. Each is about 15 lines from the module-size limit, and going over fails cargo test and reverts the whole task.
• Test with stdout pointed at /dev/full against a local stub server, plus one row checking a normal pipe stays silent.
• Run the recall control as one command, with the marker counted before the run.
• Post the before table on the ticket before editing code, and the after table before the full test gate.

Task 2: Fixes #1232 (product). This is the backlog-drain slot: 18 open self-filed issues, and this one has the least work left. A recursive delete of the parent folder currently asks only when spelled exactly ... The task normalises the path text so ../, ./.., ../.., build/../.. and ${PWD:?}/.. all ask, plus ../*. ../sibling and build/../dist must still pass. It also has to check the ticket's harm claim first. I believe, without having tested it, that POSIX rm already refuses any target ending in . or ... If so, the real danger is ../*, which deletes the parent's contents including the working folder. The implementer runs real rm in a throwaway temp folder and posts what happens before claiming any harm.

